annin Jr.</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 PIT are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.39 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>In the red zone CLE throw it (56% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>PIT \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>PIT are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Jaylen Warren</strong> (14 carries, 64 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>DK Metcalf</strong> projects for <strong>8 targets and 70 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Pat Freiermuth</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 38 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>CLE \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>CLE are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (45% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Quinshon Judkins</strong> (13 carries, 50 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong> projects for <strong>9 targets and 74 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Harold Fannin Jr.</strong> (TE, 9 targets, 66 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single bigges